# TEMPLATE ONLY. Copy to .env.local and fill that in instead.
#
# ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
# │ DO NOT PUT REAL VALUES IN THIS FILE. Two reasons, either one decisive:    │
# │                                                                           │
# │  1. It is COMMITTED. `.gitignore` ignores `.env*` and then un-ignores     │
# │     this one, because a template is meant to be shared. A key pasted      │
# │     here is a key pushed to GitHub.                                       │
# │                                                                           │
# │  2. It does not even work. Next.js loads `.env`, `.env.local`,            │
# │     `.env.development`, `.env.production` and their `.local` variants —   │
# │     never `.env.example`. A key here is read by nobody.                   │
# │                                                                           │
# │ Local values go in `.env.local` (gitignored). Production values go in     │
# │ the Vercel dashboard: Project → Settings → Environment Variables.         │
# └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Database
# "sqlite" (default) or "postgres".
#
#   LOCAL      -> sqlite. Needs no install; Node 22.5+ ships node:sqlite.
#   PRODUCTION -> postgres. REQUIRED on Vercel and every other serverless
#                 host: their filesystems are ephemeral and read-only, so a
#                 SQLite file would be wiped on each deploy and cold start,
#                 taking every booking request and wall post with it.
#
# `pg` is already installed, so switching is just these two variables.
DATABASE_DRIVER=sqlite
# Where the SQLite file lives. Gitignored. Ignored when driver=postgres.
SQLITE_PATH=.data/cluster.db
# Only read when DATABASE_DRIVER=postgres. Neon's free tier is the intended
# home; use its POOLED connection string, which is the right one for
# serverless functions.
# Format: postgresql://user:password@host/dbname?sslmode=require
DATABASE_URL=

# Salt for hashing submitter IPs on wall posts and reports. Raw IPs are never
# stored. Set this to a long random string before going live — leaving it
# unset falls back to a known dev value, which makes the hashes guessable.
IP_HASH_SALT=

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Booking email
# Booking requests always persist to the database. Email is best-effort: with
# no key the request is still saved and simply shows as un-emailed in admin.
RESEND_API_KEY=
BOOKING_EMAIL=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- Stats
#
# WHAT THE LIVE STATS WINDOW SHOWS, AND WHERE EACH FIGURE COMES FROM.
#
#   YouTube  · Subscribers ......... LIVE  (YouTube Data API v3, key below)
#            · Unique viewers ...... MANUAL — no public endpoint exists
#   Instagram· Followers ........... LIVE  (Instagram Graph API, below)
#            · Views (28d) ......... LIVE  (Instagram Graph API insights)
#   Spotify  · Monthly listeners ... MANUAL — Spotify-for-Artists only
#            · Playlist adds ....... MANUAL — Spotify-for-Artists only
#
# Every figure, live or manual, is cached server-side after it first resolves
# (a `cache:<key>` row in `manual_stats`), so an expired token or a rate limit
# shows the last real number rather than a blank. See src/lib/audience.ts.
#
# Leaving a block below empty is a supported state: that platform's figures
# simply fall through to whatever is entered in /admin.

# --- YouTube Data API v3 -------------------------------------------------
# Gives: subscriberCount, viewCount, videoCount. Public data, so an API KEY is
# enough — no OAuth, no channel owner consent.
#
# HOW TO GET IT:
#   1. console.cloud.google.com -> new project
#   2. APIs & Services -> Library -> enable "YouTube Data API v3"
#   3. Credentials -> Create credentials -> API key
#   4. Restrict the key to the YouTube Data API (and to your domain in prod)
#   5. Channel id: youtube.com/account_advanced, or the UC... in the channel URL
#
# NOT AVAILABLE HERE: "unique viewers" is a YouTube Studio (Analytics) figure
# with no equivalent metric in any public API, OAuth or otherwise. It stays a
# manual field in /admin.
YOUTUBE_API_KEY= 
# The UC... id, NOT the channel URL and NOT the @handle — `fetchYouTubeStats`
# passes this straight to the API's `&id=` parameter, which accepts neither.
YOUTUBE_CHANNEL_ID=

# --- Instagram Graph API -------------------------------------------------
# Gives: followers_count, and the `views` insight over a rolling 28 days.
#
# REQUIRES a Professional (Business or Creator) Instagram account linked to a
# Facebook Page. The old Basic Display API cannot return either figure and was
# retired; this is the Graph API.
#
# HOW TO GET IT:
#   1. Link the IG account to a Facebook Page (IG app -> Settings -> Account
#      type and tools -> Switch to professional account)
#   2. developers.facebook.com -> create an app (type: Business)
#   3. Add the "Instagram Graph API" product
#   4. Graph API Explorer -> select the app -> request permissions:
#        instagram_basic, instagram_manage_insights, pages_show_list,
#        pages_read_engagement
#   5. Generate a User token, then exchange it for a LONG-LIVED PAGE token:
#        GET /oauth/access_token?grant_type=fb_exchange_token
#            &client_id=<APP_ID>&client_secret=<APP_SECRET>
#            &fb_exchange_token=<SHORT_LIVED_TOKEN>
#        then GET /me/accounts and take the page's `access_token`
#      A long-lived Page token does not expire while it is being used, but it
#      is invalidated by a password change — if the figures freeze, this is
#      the first thing to re-issue.
#   6. Find the IG user id:
#        GET /<PAGE_ID>?fields=instagram_business_account
INSTAGRAM_USER_ID=
INSTAGRAM_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# --- Spotify -------------------------------------------------------------
# Client Credentials flow only. Everything read is public catalogue data
# (followers, popularity, releases), so there is no user authorization, no
# scopes and no redirect URI. Keep the secret server-side.
#
# NOT AVAILABLE HERE: monthly listeners and playlist adds are
# Spotify-for-Artists figures with no Web API endpoint at any access tier, and
# scraping the public artist page would breach the Developer Terms. Both stay
# manual fields in /admin.
SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=
SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Defaults to the artist id already used by the Dock if left blank.
SPOTIFY_ARTIST_ID=
# Market for release counts (ISO 3166-1 alpha-2).
SPOTIFY_MARKET=IN

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- Admin
# scrypt hash of the admin password — not the password itself.
ADMIN_PASSWORD_HASH=

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Visitor login
#
# Two ways in, one account: Google, or a six-digit code emailed to an address.
# Both end by proving the same thing — that this person can read mail at X —
# so both merge onto a single row keyed by that address.
#
# ONE SESSION COVERS BOTH PERSONAS. Signing in at the DEV door lets you
# straight through the DOTM one. That is deliberate: the cookie is not scoped
# per face.
#
# NOTHING HERE IS REQUIRED TO RUN THE SITE LOCALLY. With no Google credentials
# the sign-in button runs a local stand-in that creates a real account and a
# real session, so the gate, the analytics join, the avatar and sign-out are
# all exercised. Paste real values below and the same button becomes the real
# thing — no code change, no rebuild.
#
# The stand-in REFUSES TO ARM when NODE_ENV=production unless AUTH_DEMO_MODE=1
# is set on purpose. Forgetting a variable in production must not leave the
# front door open to anybody.

# --- Google OAuth --------------------------------------------------------
# HOW TO GET THESE:
#   1. console.cloud.google.com -> new project
#   2. APIs & Services -> OAuth consent screen -> External -> app name and
#      support email. "Testing" is fine until you go live.
#   3. Credentials -> Create credentials -> OAuth client ID -> Web application
#   4. Authorised redirect URI — the path is:
#        http://localhost:3000/api/auth/google/callback
#      Note the order: /google/callback, NOT /callback/google. Getting this
#      wrong is the single most common cause of redirect_uri_mismatch.
#      Add the production origin too when you deploy.
#   5. Copy the client id and secret here.
#
# Only `openid email profile` is requested. Nothing else is this site's
# business, and a consent screen asking for more is one people stop reading.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Only needed when the callback is not <origin>/api/auth/google/callback — for
# instance behind a proxy that rewrites the host. Leave blank otherwise.
GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=

# --- One-time codes ------------------------------------------------------
# Codes are stored as salted hashes and never as digits. They expire after ten
# minutes or five wrong guesses, whichever comes first.
#
# With no RESEND_API_KEY (set further up, shared with booking email) the code
# is PRINTED TO THE SERVER CONSOLE rather than emailed, and the sign-in panel
# says so plainly. That is a complete, walkable flow with no mail provider at
# all.
#
# `onboarding@resend.dev` works out of the box but is visibly a test sender.
# Verify your own domain in Resend and change this when you are ready.
OTP_FROM_EMAIL=

# --- Sessions ------------------------------------------------------------
# The session cookie is signed with COOKIE_SECRET, shared with the persona and
# locale cookies. Set it to a long random string before going live — unset, it
# falls back to a known development value, and a known signing key means
# anybody can mint a valid session for anybody.
COOKIE_SECRET=

# Set to 1 ONLY to allow the demo sign-in provider on a production build — a
# staging box you want walkable before Google is configured. Never on the real
# site.
AUTH_DEMO_MODE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Analytics
#
# Visits and events go into the same database as everything else. No third
# party, no external script, nothing loaded from another origin.
#
# WHAT IS RECORDED: which windows were opened, how many times, and how long
# each stayed open; route views through the boot flow; dock and taskbar
# clicks, track plays and outbound clicks; sign-in attempts and their results;
# and per visit the persona, locale, referrer, user agent and viewport.
#
# WHAT IS NOT: raw IP addresses, ever. They are salted and hashed with
# IP_HASH_SALT above, exactly as wall posts are — set that to a long random
# string or the hashes are guessable.
#
# There is nothing to configure. The tables are created on first run alongside
# the rest of the schema.

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Desktop metrics
# The two counters on the DOTM/DEV desktops are NOT environment variables.
#
# "Cluster count" is Instagram followers + YouTube subscribers, resolved
# through the same live-then-cache-then-manual chain as the Live Stats window
# (src/lib/audience.ts) — so configuring the keys above updates the wallpaper
# and the stats window together.
#
# "New cluster fam" has no endpoint behind it and stays a `manual_stats` row
# entered in /admin, updatable without a redeploy. Until set, both fall back to
# the supplied defaults in src/content/metrics.ts rather than an invented
# figure.
